{{NZelectorateShortDesc|current=yes|type=general|region=Auckland}}
'''Manurewa''' is a New Zealand parliamentary [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]] in southern [[Auckland]]. A very safe [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour]] seat, the seat was created in 1963 and has returned a [[New Zealand National Party|National]] MP only once, in [[1975 New Zealand general election|1975]]. [[Arena Williams]] has represented the electorate since the {{NZ election link|2020}}.

==Population centres==
The electorate is based around the suburb of [[Manurewa]]. It includes [[Wiri]], [[Manukau|Manukau Central]] and [[Manukau Heights]] in the north and stretches south to [[Homai]], [[Clendon Park]], [[Hillpark, Auckland|Hillpark]] and [[Weymouth, New Zealand|Weymouth]].<ref name="parllib">{{cite web| url= https://www3.parliament.nz/en/mps-and-electorates/electorate-profiles/manurewa-electorate-profile/ |title=Manurewa - Electorate Profile |publisher= Parliamentary Library |date= 27 April 2021 |access-date= 22 September 2025}}</ref>

{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Manurewa.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
In boundary changes in 2002, some areas moved to [[Clevedon (New Zealand electorate)|Clevedon]] and [[Manukau East]] electorates. In the 2007 boundary review, [[Wattle Downs]] and parts of [[Manurewa East]] moved to [[Papakura (New Zealand electorate)|Papakura electorate]]. Boundary changes for the 2008 election made Manurewa even safer for Labour, although some party loyalists were apparently upset that this had the effect of making the neighbouring seat of [[Papakura (New Zealand electorate)|Papakura]] less marginal and more inclined towards National. The 2013/14 redistribution did not change the boundaries further.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2014 |url=http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |isbn=978-0-477-10414-2 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=4 October 2014 |page=9 |date=4 April 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141006075102/http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |archive-date=6 October 2014 |url-status=dead }}</ref> The 2019/20 redistribution saw the electorate shift northwards, gaining the area north of Puhinui Road from [[Manukau East]] and losing the area east of the [[Auckland Southern Motorway|Southern Motorway]] to the new {{NZ electorate link|Takanini}} electorate.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://vote.nz/assets/Boundary-Review/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2020.pdf |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2020 |date=17 April 2020}}</ref> In the 2025 boundary review, the electorate would gain parts of [[Manurewa]] and [[Wattle Downs]] from the over-quota {{NZ electorate link|Takanini}} electorate, and part of [[Wiri]] from {{NZ electorate link|Mangere}}, while ceding an area of [[Papatoetoe]] between [[State Highway 20 (New Zealand)|State Highway 20]] and [[North Island Main Trunk]] to {{NZ electorate link|Mangere}}.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025 |url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf |isbn=978-0-473-75233-0 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=22 September 2025 |page=19 |date=8 August 2025 }}</ref>

==History==
Manurewa is generally regarded as a safe Labour seat. [[Louisa Wall]] won the Manurewa electorate in the {{NZ election link|2011}} and was returned to the [[50th New Zealand Parliament]];<ref>{{cite news |url= http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=10717554 |title=Louisa Wall back in Parliament |first=Derek |last=Cheng |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=6 April 2011 |access-date=6 April 2011}}</ref> she had opted not to be on Labour's party list that time.<ref>{{cite news |title=Louisa Wall passes on Labour's list |url= http://www.3news.co.nz/general/louisa-wall-passes-on-labours-list-2011041105 |access-date=4 October 2014 |work=[[3News]] |date=11 April 2011}}</ref> She was back on the list (in [[Party lists in the 2014 New Zealand general election#Labour Party|position 12]]) for the {{NZ election link|2014}} but won her electorate comfortably.<ref>{{cite web |url= http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2014/electorate-25.html |title=Official Count Results -- Manurewa |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date=4 October 2014 |date=4 October 2014}}</ref>

